  (text "Commander battery module (rev B0) - microcontroller and the port to the mainboard.\n\nSTC8H8K64U (the keyboard's chip: one part number, one tool chain), at 3.3 V.  It is the module's gauge and charge\ncontroller, and a USB HID 'Power Device' (UPS class) towards the mainboard: Linux reads charge, voltage, current\nand time-to-empty from it with the stock usbhid-ups / upower stack.  Firmware is new - pin table in README.md.\n\nThe port: a USB-C receptacle (16-pin, USB 2.0) cabled to the mainboard's battery port J40.\n- The module offers power: Rp (36 k to 3.3 V = 'default USB power') on both CC lines from pin CC_RP.\n- When the mainboard asks for a charge (a HID feature report), the firmware drops Rp and switches Rd (5.1 k) in\n(CC_RD), so the mainboard's FUSB302 sees a sink and may raise BATT_VBUS_EN.  The buck stays enabled meanwhile\n(it idles under the mainboard's slightly higher voltage) so a PoE drop never interrupts the 5 V." (exclude_from_sim no) (at 15.24 25.4 0)
    (effects (font (size 1.524 1.524)) (justify left top)) (uuid "d5f9aac5-babb-56c5-80b6-259f28ec2aa9"))
